---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ActionLink from '~/components/ActionLink.astro';
import PageHeader from '~/components/PageHeader.astro';
import Prose from '~/components/Prose.astro';

// Bump this when the copy below actually changes.
const LAST_UPDATED = new Date('2026-08-13');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — nebulous-code"
  description="Nicholas Licalsi — a decade across web development, product work, and senior engineering."
>
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <PageHeader
      updated={LAST_UPDATED}
      title="About"
      lede="My software is a tool to run my company and a toy to enjoy my free time. I don't abandon hard requirements when they're inconvenient, but I'll ship before my project is polished. If you want to see what I've wrestled with recently, check out the blog."
    />

    <div class="body">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          When all you've got is an IDE, everything looks like it can be fixed with software. With a decade of experience as a software professional, I've got a few more tools than an IDE. However, I still use software to optimize and customize how I engage with my career, publishing business, and hobbies.
        </p>

        <p>
          I regularly iterate through designs with coworkers, customers, and stakeholders to make sure I build them the software they need to do their jobs better. Hashing out an analytics dashboard face-to-face is the most rewarding part of my job. I get to watch an opaque corner of a business come into focus. And rather than assembling a rote report by hand, my coworkers can spend their morning analyzing the results.
        </p>

        <p>
          My wife and I run an independent publishing company, which has taught me a lot about how to operate a profitable and sustainable business. Software is a tool I use to save money on website costs and improve the production value of our books. Many of my business problems become side projects because I see an opportunity for improvement in my workflow. A satisfying result of combining my two passions is that I've built sites and tools that other authors depend on.
        </p>

        <p>
          I write code because it's fun. I write fiction because I enjoy it. I also collect Pokémon cards, solve the Rubik's cube, and play retro video games to relax when I'm not working. I build custom dashboards for my card collection, spaced-repetition algorithms for my cube practice, and emulators to understand more about retro consoles. Each of these teaches me more about my hobbies and more about software engineering.
        </p>

        <p>
          Over the years I've picked up tips, tricks, and tools. Documenting and sharing is how I cement those lessons for myself and others. I maintain a blog focused on the problems I've faced and overcome. It's written for who I was early on in my career, but many lessons still apply to what I do today.
        </p>
      </Prose>

      <div class="page-cta">
        <ActionLink href="/projects" tone="muted">see projects</ActionLink>
        <ActionLink href="/blog">read the blog</ActionLink>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .body {
    padding-block: var(--space-10);
  }

  /* Keeps the page from dead-ending after the copy. Primary sits last so it
     lands on the right edge, matching the project card footer. */
  .page-cta {
    margin-top: var(--space-9);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }
</style>
